Treecle 1.2.1
-------------
Treecle creates and manages user-defined, custom-built trees.

Whatever that means, it does sounds impressive.

Seriously, Treecle can be used to create a heirarchy of your own design,
representing whatever structure exists in the data you want to store. This
heirarchy can be stored, retrieved, expanded, etc., in any way within the
limits of the program through the treelike user interface.


Changes in version 1.2.1
------------------------
1. The filename is displayed in the title bar.
2. A visual indication (*) is added to the filename when it is modified.
3. The program begins with a new blank file with one branch already added.


Changes in version 1.2
----------------------
1. The program saves file specific lockfiles, images and other data in ~/.local/share/treecle.
When running this version for the first time, all data in the old data folder
(~/.treecle) will be copied to the new location. The program retains the old data folder,
so if you wish to delete it, this will need to be done manually.
2. Images added to the tree are now displayed resized to the width of the editor window,
(if they are larger). The image width resizes with the width of the editor window.
3. There can be multiple instances of the program, but opening and editing the same file by
different instances are caught and not permitted.
4. Code related to file saving has been modified amd the program now creates a backup
file on saving.
5. The data file format has been tweaked to keep track of images linked to a file, and
to keep backward compatibility with version 1.1.
6. Search related code has been modified. The search string is highlighted and the text
scrolls to keep in within the editor window.
7. Removing an image from a file also deletes it from the treecle data directory.

These changes seem to be significant enough for moving from the 1.1 versioning series to 1.2.

Changes in version 1.1.6
------------------------
1. Removed Webkit. This is no longer supported by the Qt toolkit above version 6.
2. Minor changes to the UI.
3. Added spellcheck.
4. Added keyboard shortcuts to navigate between the editor and the tree panels.
5. Modified lockfile behavior code, plus other bug fixes.

Changes in version 1.1.5
------------------------
1. Added code to detect modification to the data tree.

Changes in version 1.1.4
------------------------
1. If no file name is set the program asks the user to supply one.

Changes in version 1.1.3
------------------------
1. Added a File-Save-As item to the main menu.

Changes in version 1.1.2
------------------------
1. On exit, the program would ask to save the data to file even when it was not
modified. Fixed.
2. There were problems in displaying non-English characters. Fixed, hopefully.
3. Added a shortcut key sequence to quit the program.

Changes in version 1.1.1
------------------------
This version fixes some minor bugs, for which many thanks to Tim Scheit, who
has also provided the German translation for all the user-visible text. This is
incorporated in a translation file, which is used by the interface if the
language locale is German.
The toolbar layout is also modified.

Changes in version 1.1
----------------------
1. Added the facility the sort the tree in ascending or descending order
2. Added a box to the toolbar to search for and find text in the tree.
3. Modified the README and PDF help file.

Availability
------------
Treecle is available as source code to compile on your own machine.
All the source files are available on Github under the following link:
https://github.com/letapk/treecle

The repository is public. Anyone can check out the branch onto her local machine.

Compiling the source
--------------------
You will need the libraries and developement files from Qt, which are
available from the depository of your Linux distribution or from "https://www.qt.io".

You will also need the spellcheck library from Qt (libqtspell-qt6-dev) which may be available
from the package manager of your distribution.
Alternatively it can be obtained from GitHub ("https://github.com/manisandro/qtspell").
If you wish to roll your own, follow the instructions there. You may need to modify "CMakeLists.txt"
appropriately for your system to build the QtSpell libraries. After they are built, move them to
a standard location such as "/usr/local/lib64" and the QtSpell headers to "/usr/local/include".

However, if you already have libqtspell-qt6-dev, the QtSpell headers are included in the Treecle
source.

Since the spellcheck code also requires the "enchant" spell-checking library, that will have to be
installed as well. This should be available through the package manager of your distribution.

If you are using QtCreator, load the project file "treecle.pro" which is available in the source
package. Select the version you want to build (debug version or release version) and hit
Ctrl-B. If there are no problems, this should build the binary.

The binary may be stored locally in a directoriy separate from the source subdirectory,
depending on how QtCreator is configured. This location will be available within QtCreator.

If you are not using QtCreator, running the qmake6 (or qmake) command:
>qmake treecle.pro
in a terminal should create a Makefile for your system. Then, running:
>make
should create a running binary.

If you are using QtCreator, the location of the locally compiled binary will be
available from within QtCreator. From this location copy the binary to a place which
is included in the system's path. For example, in a terminal window give the command:

>sudo cp ./treecle /usr/local/bin/treecle
Enter the root password when prompted.

This copies the binary to /usr/local/bin. if you need the German language translation, then do:
>sudo cp ./treecle_de.qm /usr/local/bin/treecle_de.qm

When executing the program for the first time in any user's account, a message notifying the creation of
a subdirectory named "~/.local/share/treecle" will appear.

In order to view the online help, the presence of the help file (treeclehelp.pdf) in this location
is required.

The file COPYING, which displays information about the program and the license is also required to be
here to enable viewing it from within the utility.

The current source tree compiles and runs using Qt version 6.11 under OpenSUSE Tumbleweed with KDE Plasma
as the desktop.

